# CODEOWNERS — Claude Code Native
#
# GitHub uses this file to auto-request reviews on pull requests.
# Maintainer: @lycogrypho (the local author "Lycogrypho" / c.attanasi@grypho.it).

# Default owner for everything in the repo.
*                       @lycogrypho

# Security-sensitive policies — keep ownership explicit even though the glob
# above already matches. The last matching rule wins, so order matters here.
/SECURITY.md            @lycogrypho
/docs/adr/              @lycogrypho
/build.gradle.kts       @lycogrypho
/src/main/kotlin/dev/lain/claudejb/process/      @lycogrypho
/src/main/kotlin/dev/lain/claudejb/permission/   @lycogrypho
/src/main/kotlin/dev/lain/claudejb/protocol/     @lycogrypho

# Two files nothing may edit in passing. CLAUDE.md carries the standing directive over the
# guard and its tests, and .gitignore is what keeps credentials and local state out of the
# history in the first place — a quiet line added to either is a change to the rules, not a
# chore, so both are owned outright.
/CLAUDE.md              @lycogrypho
/.gitignore             @lycogrypho

# A workflow is privileged code: it runs with a token, and it can publish to the Marketplace. A ruleset is
# the gate that decides what reaches main at all. Both are listed LAST so they win over every earlier glob.
#
# NB what this file does today is REQUEST a review, not require one: `require_code_owner_review` is FALSE in
# both .github/rulesets/*.json, for the same reason `required_approving_review_count` is 0 — GitHub does not
# let an author approve their own pull request, so on a single-maintainer repository requiring a code-owner
# review makes the branch unmergeable rather than well-guarded. The moment a second maintainer has write
# access, turn it on in BOTH rulesets; the ownership below is already written for that day.
/.github/               @lycogrypho
/.github/workflows/     @lycogrypho
/.github/rulesets/      @lycogrypho
/scripts/               @lycogrypho
